1. Were the $2,700 in student‑loan interest payments made in tax year 2024 (the year you’re filing for)? The student‑loan interest deduction only applies to interest you actually paid during the tax year.  
2. Is the $11,200 (graphic design) self‑employment income (you received it as an independent contractor), and did the $780 unreimbursed expenses relate to that freelance work or to your W‑2 job?  
3. Is only one spouse earning the self‑employment income, or do both spouses have self‑employment income?

If you want me to assume the common case (the $2,700 was paid in 2024, the $11,200 is self‑employment income for one spouse, and the $780 are business expenses for that self‑employment), here’s a practical breakdown and recommendation.

Summary of amounts you provided
- W‑2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $79,400  
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $11,200  
- Interest: Citi savings $1,780 + E*TRADE $195 = $1,975 total interest  
- Rent: $2,400/month → $28,800/year  
- Home office: 165 sq ft exclusive of 690 sq ft total → 165/690 = 23.91% of home  
- Charitable cash gifts: $2,000 (you have receipt)  
- Student‑loan interest reported: $2,700 (see question #1 above)  
- Unreimbursed expenses: $780 (need to know whether business or employee)

How this affects deductions and filing choices

1) Standard deduction vs. itemizing (federal)
- For tax year 2024 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $29,200.  
- Your only listed itemizable deduction is the $2,000 charity (you didn’t list mortgage interest, property tax, or large state/local taxes). With only $2,000 in charitable donations, itemizing will be far below the standard deduction.  
- Recommendation: Unless you also have substantial mortgage interest, property taxes, large state/local taxes, or other deductible expenses you didn’t list, take the standard deduction.

2) Student‑loan interest
- The student‑loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (reduces AGI) up to $2,500 (generally). It phases out at higher MAGI for MFJ filers. Your total income (as shown) is about $92.6k before adjustments, which is below the typical phaseout range, so you likely can take most or all of the allowable deduction — but the maximum allowed is normally $2,500 per year, not $2,700. Also, only interest paid during the tax year is deductible.  
- Action: confirm the $2,700 were paid in 2024 and whether you received Form 1098‑E (lender). If you truly paid $2,700 in 2024, you can deduct up to the legal cap (likely $2,500) subject to MAGI rules.

3) Interest income reporting
- Your interest total is $1,975. Because total taxable interest and ordinary dividends exceed $1,500, you need to file Schedule B (Form 1040 Schedule B) to report these interest amounts.

4) Self‑employment (graphic design) and unreimbursed expenses
- Treat the $11,200 as business income on Schedule C (Form 1040), if it was earned as an independent contractor. You must report it whether or not you received any 1099s.  
- The $780 unreimbursed expenses: if these expenses were for your W‑2 job (employee), they’re not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law. If they were business expenses for the graphic design (self‑employed), they belong on Schedule C and are deductible against that business income.

5) Home office
- You said the space is exclusive (165 sq ft) out of 690 sq ft — that generally meets the exclusive and regular use test for the home‑office deduction, provided it’s used regularly and only for the business. Only a self‑employed person (Schedule C filer) or a partner/shareholder with special rules can take the deduction; W‑2 employees generally cannot.  
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 165 sqft × $5 = $825 deduction. No Form 8829 needed.  
  b) Regular (actual expense) method: allocate a percentage of actual home expenses to the business. Your rent allocable to the business = 23.91% × $28,800 = $6,888 (annual). You can also allocate other eligible expenses (utilities, renters insurance, etc.) to increase the deduction. If you use the regular method, you generally file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with Schedule C. The regular method usually gives a larger deduction here because of high rent; however:
    - The home office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from that business for the year (it cannot create or increase a business loss). If your Schedule C net profit before the home office deduction would be less than the home office allocation, excess is carried forward to future years.
    - The home office deduction reduces self‑employment net income (thus reducing self‑employment tax) because it’s a Schedule C expense.
- Recommendation: If the graphic design income is yours and you have no other business expenses large enough to reduce net profit below the home office allocation, the regular method looks more advantageous here. Keep rent receipts, a floor plan/measurement showing 165 sq ft, and utility bills.

6) Self‑employment tax and estimated payments
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment income. Use Schedule SE to compute self‑employment tax. You also get to deduct half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- If you expect to owe $1,000 or more in tax after withholding/credits, you should make quarterly estimated payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid penalties. With $11,200 gross and deductions, your estimated liability is likely modest, but check after computing net profit.

7) Other potential tax planning items to reduce tax
- If you can, pre‑tax contributions to retirement accounts (SEP IRA, Solo 401(k), Traditional IRA if eligible) will reduce AGI and tax. A SEP or Solo 401(k) could allow substantial deductions for a self‑employed person. Consider with a tax pro.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)  
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments to income: student loan interest deduction, the deductible half of SE tax shown on Schedule 1)  
- Schedule B (if interest + dividends > $1,500) — you need this because your interest is $1,975  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report $11,200 graphic design income and related business expenses (including the business share of the rent/home office if you use the regular method)  
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax on Schedule C net profit  
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method for the home office (not needed for simplified method)  
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely based on listed items)  
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) — you should receive from the lender; use it to support the student‑loan deduction

California (state) filing
- California Form 540 (or 540NR if nonresident). You’ll report federal AGI and then make California adjustments on Schedule CA (540). California generally conforms to many federal rules but there are differences — e.g., some student‑loan interest and retirement adjustments can differ. If you live in San Diego and are full‑year California residents, file Form 540 and attach necessary schedules.

