- Student loan interest paid $2,700 in 2024, but the federal deduction limit is $2,500 (see below).
- Charitable cash gift $2,000 (you have receipt).

What is deductible (federal)
- W‑2 wages: report on Form 1040 (W‑2).
- Self‑employment income: report gross $11,200 on Schedule C; deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (your $780) on Schedule C.
- Home‑office (self‑employed): deductible on Schedule C either by:
  - Simplified method: $5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 165 × $5 = $825. No Form 8829 required. Report on Schedule C.
  - Regular method: allocate a percentage of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc.) to the business. With rent only: $28,800 × 23.913% ≈ $6,880. You’d use Form 8829 for the regular method. The home‑office deduction cannot exceed the net profit limit (see below).
  Note: The home office deduction is only available to self‑employed/schedule C taxpayers; not available for W‑2 employees (unless very rare exceptions).
- Interest/Dividends: taxable — report on Schedule B if total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500. Your total $1,975 > $1,500 → file Schedule B.
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 is an adjustment to income (above‑the‑line deduction) on Schedule 1, subject to income phase‑outs. You reported $2,700 paid; the maximum deductible for federal purposes is $2,500 (if you’re eligible).
- Charitable contribution $2,000: deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A (cash gift with receipt). If you take the standard deduction you do not deduct it on the federal return (no “universal” above‑the‑line charity deduction for 2024).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses ($780): NOT deductible for federal income tax (suspended through 2025 by TCJA) if these are employee expenses. If that $780 is actually self‑employed business expense, deduct it on Schedule C (we assumed that).
- Self‑employment tax: you must pay SE tax on your net self‑employment earnings (Schedule SE). Half of the self‑employment tax is an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

Important limits and notes
- Home‑office limit: Under the regular method, the deductible home‑office amount cannot exceed your net profit from that business (gross business income minus other business expenses). In your case, gross $11,200 − other expenses $780 = $10,420 is the available pre‑home‑office profit, so a $6,880 home‑office deduction would be allowed (it does not exceed $10,420).
- Student loan interest phaseout: there is a MAGI phaseout for the student‑loan interest deduction. For most couples with AGI in the ranges typically well below high income thresholds, the $2,500 full deduction is allowed — but you should confirm your MAGI against IRS limits for 2024. (Based on the example calculations below, you are well below common phaseout thresholds and likely eligible for the full $2,500.)
- State rules (California): California tax law differs from federal in some respects (e.g., CA may not conform to the federal student loan interest adjustment). You must check CA Form 540 instructions or a tax pro about state adjustments — expect to add back any federal above‑the‑line adjustments that California disallows.

