Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which tax forms you’ll likely need. I’m not a CPA — this is general guidance; if you want binding advice or to optimize complex items I recommend a tax professional. A few quick clarifying questions (below) will let me give slightly more precise answers.

Quick summary (short answer)
- Likely better to take the 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly ($29,200) because your stated itemizable items (charity $2,000 plus unknown state/local taxes, mortgage interest, etc.) are probably less than the standard deduction.
- Still claim the student-loan interest adjustment (up to $2,500 — you paid $2,700 so you’ll likely get the $2,500 max unless your MAGI phases you out).
- Report the $11,200 graphic-design income on Schedule C (and pay self‑employment tax via Schedule SE). You can deduct legitimate business expenses (including an allowable home-office deduction).
- Home-office: you can use either the simplified method (165 sq ft × $5 = $825) or the actual method (percentage of rent/utilities). Because your rent is high relative to your freelance income, the simplified method may be safer and simpler — the actual method (about 23.9% of rent = ~$6,888) could be limited by business income rules and may create a Schedule C loss that gets suspended — discuss with a preparer if you think you can legitimately take the larger actual amount.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (the $780) are NOT deductible for most taxpayers under current law (2018–2025 suspension), unless they fall into a very narrow exception.
- Interest/Dividends from Citi and E*TRADE should be reported (Form 1099-INT/1099-DIV/1099-B as applicable).

Detailed breakdown and numbers
1) Income to report
- W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $79,400 — reported on Form 1040 (W-2).
- Self-employment (graphic design): $11,200 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Report gross and subtract ordinary & necessary business expenses.
- Interest: $1,780 (Citi) and $195 (E*TRADE) — report on 1040 (from 1099-INT / 1099-DIV / 1099-B as issued).

Total gross income (simple sum): 79,400 + 11,200 + 1,780 + 195 = $92,575 (this is before business expense deductions, SE tax adjustments, etc.)

2) Student loan interest
- You reported $2,700 paid; the student-loan interest deduction limit is $2,500 (if you qualify). This is an above-the-line adjustment (reported on Schedule 1 of Form 1040) and reduces your AGI.
- Eligibility: phased out at higher MAGI thresholds (if your modified AGI is above the IRS limit you may not get the full deduction). For MFJ there is a phaseout — check your MAGI or ask a preparer to confirm.

3) Charitable contribution
- $2,000 to St. Mary’s Food Bank — deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep receipts (you have one).
- If you take the standard deduction, you cannot deduct this on Schedule A.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- Most unreimbursed employee business expenses are suspended (not deductible) through tax year 2025. If these are actually self-employment (business) expenses, they should go on Schedule C. If they’re unreimbursed W-2 employee expenses, they’re not deductible for most taxpayers.

5) Home office deduction
- Space: 165 sq ft exclusive use; house = 690 sq ft → business-use percentage = 165 / 690 ≈ 23.9%.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method = $5 × square footage = 165 × $5 = $825 (easy, no records of rent/utilities required beyond proof of eligibility).
  b) Actual method = business-use % × allocable rent & utilities. Example of rent portion only: 23.913% × $28,800 annual rent = ~$6,888. You could also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. BUT:
     - The home-office deduction under the actual method cannot create or increase a deductible business loss beyond business income rules (excess may be suspended and carried forward).
     - Because your freelance income is relatively small ($11,200), the large actual home-office amount might exceed allowable limits this year — simplified method ($825) is simpler and often safer when gross self-employment income is modest.
- To claim the home office you must use the space exclusively and regularly for your trade or business and it must be your principal place of business (or meet other IRS rules).
- Home-office deduction is taken on Schedule C (it reduces your business net profit, which affects income tax and self-employment tax).

6) Self‑employment tax
- You will owe self-employment (SE) tax on net Schedule C earnings (Schedule SE). SE tax is ~15.3% on net SE income (with a small adjustment). Half of SE tax is deductible as an adjustment on your Form 1040 (Schedule 1).

7) Standard deduction vs itemize
- Standard deduction (2024 MFJ): $29,200.
- Likely itemizable items you’ve listed: charity $2,000 plus any state/local taxes, mortgage interest, property tax, major medical, etc. You did not list property tax or mortgage interest, so from what you’ve given, itemized total is very likely less than $29,200.
- Recommendation: take the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable deductions (large state income or property taxes, mortgage interest, unreimbursed medical above the floor, etc.) that push your total above $29,200.

8) Forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for student loan interest adjustment and other adjustments
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $11,200 and business expenses, home-office deduction (or Form 8829 if using actual method; simplified method reported directly on Schedule C)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — to calculate SE tax
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize
- W-2 from Riverside Hospital
- 1099-INT, 1099-DIV, 1099-B (from Citi / E*TRADE) as applicable
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC if your clients issued them (you must report the income even if no 1099 was issued)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual method for the home office

9) Recordkeeping and practical steps
- Keep receipts for charitable donation and business expenses.
- Keep records for home office (floor plan/photos and records of rent/utilities for actual method).
- Keep 1099s, W-2, mortgage interest statements (Form 1098) and property tax bills if you have them.
- If you expect to owe tax because of self-employment income, consider estimated quarterly payments to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Consider using tax software or a CPA to make sure home office limits, student loan phaseouts, and SE tax are correctly handled — especially if using the actual home-office method or if the home-office deduction would create/sustain a business loss.

Questions I need to be more precise
- Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for your freelance graphic design (Schedule C) or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job? If they are business expenses, include them on Schedule C.
- Do you have other itemizable items (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state income tax payments, medical expenses, etc.) not listed?
- Do your two clients issue 1099-NEC (or did they)? And do you have 1099-INT / 1099-DIV / 1099-B statements from Citi and E*TRADE?
- Do you want me to draft an email summarizing these numbers to a tax preparer or save a draft for your records?

If you want, I can:
- Show a worked example computing net Schedule C income, SE tax, and estimated taxable income under both home-office methods (simplified vs actual) so you can compare, or
- Draft an email to your tax preparer listing these items and asking for confirmation; or
- Save a plain-text summary to a draft email you can send.

Which would you like next?

