- Interest/dividends: Report all interest/dividend income ($1,975 total). Because combined interest/dividends exceed $1,500, you’ll likely need Schedule B (interest & ordinary dividends).
- Self‑employment business expenses: You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses related to your graphic design (supplies, software, advertising, telephone portion, etc.) on Schedule C. That includes the portion of your rent/utilities attributable to the home office (see below).
- Self-employment tax deduction: You can deduct one-half of your self‑employment tax (an adjustment on Form 1040).

3) Home office — how to treat it and which method to use
You qualify for the home office deduction only for the self‑employment activity, not for your W‑2 work. Two methods:

A. Simplified method
- $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
- For 165 sq ft → 165 × $5 = $825 deduction.
- Easier to use, no Form 8829 needed.

B. Regular (actual expense) method
- Percentage = home office sq ft / total home sq ft = 165 / 690 ≈ 23.913% (~23.9%).
