I can help — below is a clear, practical breakdown of how each item you listed is treated for federal tax (2024 tax year), what choices you’ll have, and the forms you’ll likely need. I’ll also flag a few quick questions so I can refine calculations if you want a precise estimate.

Key takeaways up front
- For 2024, the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly (MFJ) is $29,200. With only $2,000 of known itemizable charitable contributions and no other itemized amounts listed (mortgage interest, big property tax, etc.), you will almost certainly take the standard deduction on your federal return.
- Your $11,200 graphic-design income is self‑employment income and must be reported on Schedule C. That opens up business deductions (including a home office deduction if the space qualifies).
- Student loan interest (up to $2,500) is an “above-the-line” adjustment to income (Schedule 1) — it reduces your AGI if you’re under the MAGI phaseout for that deduction.
- Interest from Citi and E*TRADE is taxable; because total interest (~$1,975) exceeds $1,500, you’ll use Schedule B.
- You will owe self-employment tax on net self-employment earnings and will use Schedule SE. Half of the SE tax is an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses for a W-2 job are generally NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended), unless you’re in a special category. If the $780 is business-related for your self-employment work, it is deductible on Schedule C.

Detailed treatment of each item
1) W-2 (Riverside Hospital) — $79,400
- Reported on Form 1040 (W-2 income).
- If your employer withheld federal tax, that will be credited against your tax due.

2) Graphic design income — $11,200
- Report gross receipts on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C. Examples: supplies, software, advertising, client-related travel, and if applicable, the home office deduction (see below).
- You’ll pay self-employment tax on net profit (Schedule SE). Self-employment tax is roughly 15.3% of net earnings (subject to the SE calculation), but you get to deduct half of the SE tax on Schedule 1.

3) Interest income — $1,780 (Citi savings) + $195 (E*TRADE) = $1,975
- Taxable interest; report on Form 1040.
- Because total interest > $1,500, complete Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).

4) Home office — 165 sq ft exclusive; total home 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month ($28,800/year)
- Qualification: it must be used regularly and exclusively for your business (you said exclusive — that’s good). It must be your principal place of business (or used to meet clients, etc.).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft (2024 simplified rate) up to 300 sq ft. For 165 sq ft → 165 * $5 = $825 deduction on Schedule C.
  b) Actual-expense method: allocate a business percentage = 165 / 690 ≈ 23.91%. You then deduct that percent of allowable home expenses. On rent alone: $28,800 * 23.91% ≈ $6,890–$6,900. You would also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you want to use actual method.
- Important limits:
  - The home office deduction cannot create a business loss for the self-employed taxpayer beyond certain passive loss rules; excess home-office amounts may be limited by net profit from the business (excess may be carried forward in many cases).
  - If you use the actual method, you complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C. Simplified method requires no Form 8829 (you still report the amount on Schedule C).
- If you have substantial rent and the office % is ~24%, the actual method likely yields a much larger deduction than the simplified method — but it’s limited by your net profit from the graphic-design business.

5) Student loan interest — $2,700 (Cornerstone, loans from 2019–2020)
- The deduction is up to $2,500 per return (above-the-line adjustment on Schedule 1) if your Modified AGI is below the phaseout threshold. If your MAGI is above the range, the deduction phases out and may be disallowed.
- Based on the incomes you listed (W-2 $79,400 + SE income ~11,200 + interest ~1,975 = ~92k before adjustments), you are likely under the phaseout and can take the deduction — but final eligibility depends on MAGI after adjustments (e.g., after half of SE tax, retirement contributions, etc.). I can calculate more precisely with your final numbers.

6) Charitable contributions — $2,000 (receipt)
- Deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). No above-the-line deduction available for general cash gifts in normal circumstances.
- Since your likely itemized total is small relative to the MFJ standard deduction ($29,200), you’ll almost certainly take the standard deduction instead of itemizing.

7) Unreimbursed expenses — $780
- If these are expenses related to your W-2 employment, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended).
- If they are business expenses for your self-employment (graphic design), report them on Schedule C as ordinary and necessary business expenses.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (student loan interest deduction, half SE tax deductible).
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — to report your graphic-design business income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax on net Schedule C profit.
- Schedule B — to report interest income (total interest > $1,500).
- Form 8829 — only if you use the actual-expense home-office method (not required for simplified method).
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely given the numbers you provided).
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — to claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20%) if you qualify; eligibility and the simplified form depend on your taxable income and the type of business. (You may be eligible for some QBI if you have net Schedule C income — I can check this if you want.)
- California state return: CA Form 540 (or the appropriate CA filing form). State rules differ from federal rules (e.g., some adjustments and credits differ), so you’ll also prepare CA schedules required by the state.

Self‑employment tax and example estimate (illustrative)
- If the $780 + home office (simplified $825) are business expenses, total business deductions = $1,605.
- Net self-employment income ≈ $11,200 − $1,605 = $9,595.
- Self-employment tax (approx) uses 92.35% of net earnings × 15.3% ≈ net × 14.13% → 9,595 × 0.1413 ≈ $1,357 SE tax.
- Half of that SE tax (~$678) is an above-the-line deduction on Schedule 1.
- Student loan interest ($2,700) is another above-the-line deduction (if eligible).
- These adjustments reduce your AGI before applying the standard deduction.

Other notes and planning items
- Estimated tax payments: because of SE tax and additional income, you may owe tax beyond what was withheld. If you expect to owe $1,000+ when you file, consider making estimated quarterly payments for the current year (Form 1040-ES) to avoid penalties.
- QBI (Section 199A) may give a deduction equal to up to 20% of qualified business income from your Schedule C. Eligibility depends on taxable income and whether your business is a specified service trade or business; I can check that after we compute taxable income.
- State (California) rules: CA has its own forms and some differences (e.g., standard deduction is much lower and CA does not follow all federal adjustments identically). You’ll file CA Form 540; let me know if you want help with CA-specific effects.

Clarifying questions (so I can run exact numbers)
1. Is the $11,200 your gross receipts? Do you already have other business expenses (supplies, software, subscriptions, hosting, advertising) in addition to the $780 and the home office? If so, list them and amounts.
2. Is the $780 unreimbursed expense related to your W-2 hospital job or to the graphic-design work?
3. Do you pay mortgage interest or property taxes, or have large state/local tax payments that might push itemized deductions above the standard deduction?
4. Do you expect any of the graphic-design clients to issue 1099-NEC or other information returns? (Either way, report the full amount.)
5. Would you like me to run a precise estimated federal tax calculation (including SE tax and whether QBI applies) using your numbers and either the simplified or actual home-office method?

If you want I can:
- Run the two computation scenarios (simplified vs actual home-office) and show estimated federal tax, SE tax, and whether itemizing would ever be beneficial.
- Draft an email you can send to a tax preparer with all relevant numbers and supporting questions.
- Prepare a checklist of documents and receipts to gather before filing (W-2, 1099s, bank and broker statements, student loan interest statement 1098-E, receipts for charitable contributions, business receipts, rent statements, utility amounts).

Which would you like next? If you want the number estimates, please answer the clarifying questions (especially whether the $780 is business-related and whether there are other business expenses).

