- Interest: $1,780 (Citi savings) + $195 (E*TRADE) = $1,975
- Rent: $2,400/month = $28,800/year for 690 sq ft
- Home office: 165 sq ft exclusive use
- Deductions/other: $2,700 student‑loan interest paid, $2,000 cash charitable donation (receipt), $780 unreimbursed employee expenses

How each item is treated (federal)
- W‑2 wages
  - Report on Form 1040 using information from your W‑2.

- Self‑employment income (graphic design $11,200)
  - Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
  - Pay self‑employment (SE) tax (Schedule SE). SE tax is roughly 15.3% on net self‑employment earnings (after the 7.65% adjustment — Schedule SE performs the math).
  - You can deduct one‑half of your SE tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).

- Interest income ($1,975)
  - Taxable interest (report on Form 1040). Because your total interest > $1,500, you will also file Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).

- Home office (165 sq ft exclusive)
  - Because you have self‑employment income, you can take a home‑office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business and it’s your principal place of business (or meets other IRS tests).
  - Two methods:
    1. Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 165 sq ft that’s 165 × $5 = $825 for the year.
    2. Actual (regular) method: allocate actual home expenses by percentage of business use. Your business percentage = 165 / 690 ≈ 23.91%. That percentage of allowable home costs (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.) is deductible on Schedule C (if you rent, the rent portion is deductible; mortgage interest would be handled differently if you owned).
       - Example using just rent: 23.91% × $28,800 = about $6,888. (If you use actual method you should include your share of utilities and other allocable expenses; you’ll generally use Form 8829 to compute and document this.)
  - Important limits:
    - The home‑office deduction for a sole proprietor cannot create or increase a net loss from the business for some passive activity or other limitation rules; the home‑office deduction is limited to the net profit from the business (excess can generally be carried forward in some cases). You must have documentation (floor plan, lease, receipts).
    - Use the method that gives the larger legitimate deduction (actual method often larger for renters, but requires more records and Form 8829).

- Unreimbursed employee expenses ($780)
  - For most employees, miscellaneous itemized deductions for unreimbursed employee business expenses were suspended by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act (through 2025). They are generally not deductible for federal returns unless you fall into a very limited category (e.g., certain performing artists or qualifying reservists). So these are likely nondeductible.

- Student‑loan interest ($2,700 paid)
  - The student‑loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (you report it on Schedule 1). The maximum deduction is normally $2,500 for the year (so you’d be limited to $2,500 of the $2,700 you paid).
  - This deduction phases out at higher MAGI levels for married filing jointly; you’ll need to check current phaseout thresholds for 2024 to confirm whether you can take the full $2,500. Given the income described (W‑2 + interest + net business income), you are likely under typical phaseout thresholds, but you should verify with exact MAGI calculations.

- Charitable contributions ($2,000, have receipt)
  - Charitable gifts are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Cash contributions to qualified charities are deductible (keep the receipt).
  - Compare total itemized deductions (charity + state/local income/taxes up to the SALT cap of $10,000 + mortgage interest, medical > threshold, etc.) to the standard deduction for married filing jointly for the 2024 year. Unless your other itemized categories (SALT, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.) are large, the standard deduction will usually be larger than $2,000.

Itemize vs standard (quick guidance)
- For 2023 the standard deduction for MFJ was $27,700; for 2024 it will be larger (inflation adjustments). You should use the official 2024 amount when preparing the return.
- Based on the numbers you provided (only $2,000 charitable plus whatever state taxes you paid), you will likely be better off taking the standard deduction. Even adding the maximum SALT ($10,000) to your $2,000 charity gives $12,000 — still below the standard deduction. So most likely: take the standard deduction.
- Exception: if you have significant mortgage interest, high state taxes, or large deductible medical expenses (above the threshold), itemizing could make sense.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and part of SE tax adjustments, among other items
- Schedule B — if total interest and ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 (your interest ~ $1,975)
- Schedule C — report self‑employment business income and business expenses (graphic design)
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home, if you use the actual (regular) method for the home office (if you use the simplified method, you generally compute the $5/sqft deduction and enter it per instructions on Schedule C)
- Form 1098‑E — you should receive this from the loan servicer documenting student‑loan interest paid (used to substantiate the student‑loan interest deduction)
- Any 1099s or other information returns (1099‑NEC from clients if issued, 1099‑INT from banks, 1099‑B for brokerage if selling investments)

California state return
- File CA Form 540 (or other CA return form for MFJ). California largely starts with federal AGI and has its own adjustments — check CA differences (for example, CA may not allow some federal above‑the‑line deductions in the same way). Confirm the student loan interest and home office treatment on the California return; CA conformity is not always identical to federal.

Estimated taxes / payments
- Because you have self‑employment income, you may need to make estimated quarterly tax payments (federal and state) if you expect to owe tax beyond withholdings. Also plan for SE tax. If you haven’t been making estimated payments, consider paying an estimate to avoid penalties.

Recordkeeping you should assemble before filing
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital
- 1099‑NEC/other records of the $11,200 (even if you didn’t receive 1099s, you must report the income)
- 1099‑INT(s) from Citi and E*TRADE
- 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from Cornerstone
- Receipts for the $2,000 charitable donation
- Lease agreement, rent receipts, utility bills, floor plan/photos, and any receipts for home expenses (if using the actual method for the home office)
- Records/documentation of the $780 in unreimbursed expenses (in case any narrow exception applies)
- Any other records of state tax paid (for SALT calculation), mortgage interest (Form 1098) if applicable

Illustrative comparison (approximate)
- Total income: 79,400 + 11,200 + 1,975 = $92,575
- If you use the simplified home‑office method ($825) you’ll have higher net business income (≈ $10,375) → larger SE tax.
- If you use actual allocation of rent (≈ $6,888) your net business income becomes ≈ $4,312, lowering both income tax and SE tax; actual method often gives more tax savings when you rent and have a sizeable exclusive office area.
- Student loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 (so you can only deduct up to that amount, not the full $2,700) on the federal return assuming you’re under the phaseout.

Recommendations / next steps
1. Gather the forms and records listed above (W‑2, 1099s, 1098‑E, charitable receipt, lease, rent receipts).
2. Decide which home‑office method to use:
   - Quick route: simplified method ($5 per sq ft = $825).
   - More beneficial but requires paperwork: actual method. Run the numbers (include rent, utilities, insurance) — actual will likely give a larger deduction in your scenario because 165/690 is a high percentage of the rental cost.
   - If using the actual method prepare to complete Form 8829.
3. Compare itemized deductions (all categories) vs the standard deduction for 2024 and take whichever yields the larger deduction — likely standard, but check SALT and any mortgage interest.
4. Use tax software or consult a CPA/tax preparer, especially because:
   - You have a mixture of W‑2 and self‑employment income (Schedule C + Schedule SE).
   - Home‑office treatment can have implications for audits and for state return conformity.
