Short upfront recommendation
- With the information you provided, you will almost certainly take the 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly (2024 MFJ standard = $29,200). The charitable gift you listed ($2,000) is far below that threshold and you don’t appear to have other itemizable amounts high enough to beat the standard deduction. The home-office deduction is available for your self‑employment (graphic design) and — using the regular method — will almost certainly be larger than the simplified option.

A. Income and quick math (approx.)
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self-employed graphic design gross: $11,200
  - Business expenses you listed (assumed applicable to the business): $780
  - Net self-employment profit (before SE tax): 11,200 − 780 = $10,420
- Interest: Citi savings $1,780 + E*TRADE $195 = $1,975
- Total gross income: 79,400 + 10,420 + 1,975 = $91,795

Self-employment tax and above-the-line deductions (approx.)
- SE taxable amount = 10,420 × 92.35% ≈ $9,626
- Self-employment tax ≈ $9,626 × 15.3% ≈ $1,473
- Half of SE tax (above-the-line deduction) ≈ $736 (this reduces AGI)
- Student loan interest: you paid $2,700, but the maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (and it phases out at higher MAGI). Given estimated AGI below phaseout, you can likely claim the full $2,500.
- Total above-the-line adjustments ≈ $736 + $2,500 = $3,236

Estimated AGI
- AGI ≈ 91,795 − 3,236 = $88,559 (rounded)

B. Home office — two methods and recommended choice
You said: home office = 165 sq ft exclusively; home = 690 sq ft; you pay $2,400/month rent.

- Business-use percentage = 165 / 690 ≈ 23.91%

1) Simplified method
- $5 × 165 sq ft = $825 (simple, no Form 8829).

2) Regular method (allocation of actual expenses)
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800
- Rent portion allocable to home office ≈ $28,800 × 23.91% ≈ $6,888
- If you have utilities, renters’ insurance, internet (business portion), those prorated amounts would add to the deductible home‑office expenses under the regular method. You would report this on Form 8829 and carry to Schedule C.

Recommendation: regular method almost certainly gives a larger deduction (≈ $6,888 just from rent vs. $825 simplified). Note two important limitations:
- The home‑office deduction for a self‑employed person cannot exceed the net profit from that business. Your pre-home-office net profit is about $10,420, so a home‑office deduction of ~$6,888 is allowable (it doesn’t exceed profit).
- If you were an employee doing design work but not self‑employed, the home office deduction is generally not available (unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible in 2018–2025).

C. Itemize vs. standard deduction
- 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $29,200.
- Known itemizable amounts you listed: charitable cash = $2,000. (SALT, mortgage interest, medical >7.5% of AGI, etc. — you did not list).
- Unless you have additional large deductible items (large state and local taxes, mortgage interest, sizable medical expenses, or other unreimbursed qualifying items), your itemized total is well below $29,200. Therefore the standard deduction is almost certainly better.

D. Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — adjustments to income (student loan interest if applicable; other adjustments).
- Schedule B — interest/dividend reporting (you have $1,975 interest; Schedule B is required if over $1,500).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — your graphic design income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax.
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the regular method for the home office). If you use the simplified method, you claim the deduction on Schedule C without Form 8829.
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction, if applicable (may reduce taxable income by up to 20% of qualified business income; eligibility depends on taxable income and type of business).
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely here).
- California state tax: CA Form 540 (you’ll also report state taxable income, and CA has its own treatment; estimated payments may be needed for state).

E. Other items and practical notes
- Student loan interest: you reported $2,700 paid; the allowable deduction is capped at $2,500. Also this deduction phases out at higher MAGI — with your numbers you are well below likely phaseout thresholds, so full $2,500 should be allowed.
- Charitable: keep the receipt (you said you have it). Cash gifts are deductible only if you itemize.
- 1099s / W-2 / 1099-INT: gather W-2; any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC from the two graphic-design clients; 1099-INTs for interest — these support the numbers above.
- Estimated tax payments: because you have self‑employment income, you may need to make quarterly estimated payments for federal and state tax to avoid underpayment penalties.
- QBI deduction: you may be eligible for a QBI (Section 199A) deduction for the self‑employment income. That’s commonly useful; I can compute an estimate if you want.
- Retirement contributions: consider contributing to a SEP-IRA / Solo 401(k) to reduce taxable income if you want retirement savings and tax deferral for 2024 (subject to limits and timing). If interested I can show rough numbers.

F. Next steps / questions for me to refine your return estimate
