I’m not a tax professional, but I can walk you through how these items are normally handled and give an estimate so you know what to expect. I’ll highlight what’s likely deductible, how to treat the home office, whether itemizing makes sense, and which tax forms you’ll typically need. You should confirm final numbers with a CPA or tax preparer.

Summary of the numbers you gave
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (CA, San Diego)  
- W-2 wages (Riverside Hospital): $79,400  
- Self‑employment (graphic design, 2 clients): $11,200  
- Interest: Citi savings $1,780 + E*TRADE $195 = $1,975 total taxable interest  
- Home: rented, $2,400/month → $28,800/year; home size 690 sq ft; exclusive home office 165 sq ft  
- Charitable cash gifts: $2,000 (you have receipt)  
- Student loan interest paid: $2,700 (Cornerstone) — note limit applies (see below)  
- “Unreimbursed expenses” $780 — need to know whether these are employee unreimbursed expenses (for your W‑2 job) or business expenses for your graphic design clients

What you can (generally) deduct
- Self‑employment business expenses: Deduct ordinary & necessary expenses for the graphic design work on Schedule C. That includes the $780 if those are business expenses (receipts/documentation recommended).  
- Home office: If the 165 sq ft is used regularly and exclusively for the business, you can take a home office deduction for the self‑employment business. Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft → 165 sq ft × $5 = $825. (No Form 8829 needed.)
  - Actual expenses method: allocate a share of rent, utilities, etc., by business-use percentage (165 ÷ 690 ≈ 23.91%). Example: rent $28,800 × 23.91% ≈ $6,888 allocated to the business (plus a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). If you use actual expenses you generally complete Form 8829 (if filing as a sole proprietor on Schedule C). The home‑office deduction reduces net profit from the business. Note: home-office deduction must be limited to net profit from that business in some situations.  
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax on net self‑employment earnings; half of the SE tax is an adjustment to income (deductible on Schedule 1).  
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 is deductible as an “above‑the‑line” adjustment to income (not an itemized deduction), subject to income limits/phaseouts and other rules. If you are married filing jointly, there are MAGI phaseouts that can reduce/eliminate the deduction if your income is high — check current MAGI phaseout thresholds for the tax year and/or confirm with a tax professional. You stated $2,700 paid; the maximum deductible amount is $2,500 if eligible.  
- Charitable contributions: $2,000 cash deductible if you itemize (reported on Schedule A). If you do not itemize you generally don’t get a federal itemized deduction for charity (except limited special rules that have been temporary in past years). You do have the receipt — keep it.

What you cannot deduct (generally)
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (for your W‑2 hospital job) — miscellaneous employee expenses were suspended under current law (through 2025) and are not deductible on your federal return unless you qualify under a few narrow exceptions. If the $780 are business expenses for your self‑employment, they are deductible on Schedule C. Clarify which they are.

Interest income and reporting
- Your total taxable interest is $1,975. If interest/dividends exceed $1,500, you typically must file Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends). So you will likely need Schedule B.

Itemize vs. standard deduction
- For 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is substantially larger than in prior years (you should confirm the exact current-year amount for 2024 with the IRS or your preparer). Based on the items you listed:
  - Your only clear itemized amount is the $2,000 charitable gift (you didn’t list mortgage interest, property tax, or large state/local taxes).  
  - Unless you have additional deductible items (significant mortgage interest, large state/property taxes, medical expenses above threshold, or other itemizable items) your itemized total is almost certainly far less than the standard deduction.  
Conclusion: It’s very likely you should take the standard deduction. If you have substantial mortgage interest or property tax (SALT) up to the $10,000 SALT cap, that could change the decision; otherwise standard deduction is almost certainly better.

Example rough tax math (illustrative)
- Business (graphic design) gross: $11,200  
- If you use the actual home‑office allocation of rent only (165/690 × $28,800 ≈ $6,888) + $780 business expenses = $7,668 total business expenses → Net self‑employment profit ≈ $11,200 − $7,668 = $3,532.  
- Self‑employment tax on that (approx): net × 0.9235 × 15.3% ≈ $499; half SE tax deductible ≈ $250.  
- Student loan interest adjustment (max allowed if eligible): $2,500 (you paid $2,700 but max is $2,500) — subject to MAGI phaseouts.  
- Interest income: $1,975 (reportable)  
- AGI would be roughly: 79,400 (W‑2) + 3,532 (net SE) + 1,975 (interest) − 250 (half SE) − 2,500 (student loan interest if eligible) ≈ $82,157 (very approximate).  
- Then you subtract the standard deduction (likely larger than your itemized total) to get taxable income. This example shows the home office deduction reduces your business income and therefore your SE tax and AGI.

Forms you will likely need
- Form 1040 (main return)  
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income) — for things like the student‑loan interest above-the-line deduction and the deductible half of self‑employment tax (depending on the year’s layout)  
- Schedule B — if interest > $1,500 (your total interest $1,975 → Schedule B is required)  
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the graphic design income and business expenses (including home office deduction if you use simplified or claim related expenses here)  
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax  
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual expense method to figure the home office deduction (not needed if you use the simplified method)  
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (probably not in your case unless you have other large deductions)  
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — for Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of qualified business income) if applicable; even with modest QBI you may be eligible but rules and phaseouts apply

Other practical notes & recommendations
- 1099s: confirm you receive (and collect) 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from clients as appropriate and 1099‑INT for interest. Report all income even if you don’t receive a 1099.  
- Keep receipts and records: for charitable gifts (your $2,000 receipt), business expenses, student‑loan interest statements (Form 1098‑E or lender statement), and records documenting exclusive home office use.  
- Clarify the $780: tell me whether it’s expenses related to your self‑employed graphic design work (Schedule C) or unreimbursed employee expenses for the W‑2 job (generally nondeductible). If they’re employee-related, they don’t help on your federal return under current law.  
- Estimated taxes / withholding: because you have self‑employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes next year (if you expect similar self‑employment income). Consider adjusting withholding or making quarterly payments to avoid penalties.  
- State taxes (California): California has different rules for some items (e.g., student loan interest treatment generally similar, but check state rules). Also property taxes and SALT limits interact with federal rules. Confirm with a CA tax pro.

Next steps I can help with
- I can prepare a checklist or a one‑page summary you can send to your CPA (or draft an email to a tax preparer listing the numbers and asking them to prepare the return).  
- If you want, tell me whether the $780 is a business expense or an employee expense and whether you have mortgage interest/property tax or other large deductions; I’ll update the estimate and say definitively whether itemizing could make sense.  
- I can also generate a draft message to your tax preparer with the amounts and documents you have.

Would you like me to:
1) Draft a concise email to your CPA with these numbers and ask them to prepare the return, or  
2) Recalculate with the simplified home‑office method (so you can see the difference), or  
3) Build a checklist of the documents to gather for filing?

