Thanks — this is enough to give a clear, practical plan. I’ll summarize what’s deductible, how the home office works, whether you should likely itemize, what forms you’ll need, and a few follow-up questions so I can refine the numbers.

Quick summary / recommendation
- Most likely you will take the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly) rather than itemizing, because the only confirmed itemized amount you gave is $2,000 of charitable gifts and that’s well below the standard deduction. (The 2024 standard deduction for MFJ is $29,200.)
- Your freelance (graphic design) income should be reported on Schedule C. Because you have a home office used exclusively for that business, you can take a home-office deduction against the self-employment income. That deduction will reduce your Schedule C net profit and therefore your self-employment tax.
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (adjustment to income), limited to $2,500 (subject to income phaseouts). If your loan and interest qualify, you can deduct up to $2,500 on Schedule 1.
- The $780 “unreimbursed expenses” are deductible only if they are business expenses for the self‑employment work (they are NOT deductible if they are employee unreimbursed expenses). If they’re business-related, put them on Schedule C.

Detailed breakdown and calculations (estimates)
1) Income (calendar year totals you provided)
- W-2 wages: $79,400
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $11,200
- Interest from Citi savings: $1,780
- Interest/dividends from E*TRADE: $195
Total gross income ≈ $92,575

2) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction (if the loan is a qualified student loan and the interest you gave is the 2024 interest paid) is limited to $2,500. You reported $2,700 — so the maximum deductible amount is $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts). With the income you listed (~$92.6k) and filing MFJ, you are likely below the phaseout threshold, so you’ll probably get the full $2,500 — but confirm the loan is a qualified student loan and that the $2,700 is interest paid in 2024.

3) Home office calculation (actual-expense method)
- Home size = 690 sq ft; office = 165 sq ft → business-use percentage = 165 / 690 ≈ 23.91%.
- Rent = $2,400/month → annual rent = $28,800.
- Rent portion allocable to the home office ≈ 28,800 × 23.91% ≈ $6,886.94.
- If you use the actual-expense method for the home office, that $6,886.94 (plus any business portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you have them) is a Schedule C expense. You should also add the $780 if those are business-related expenses. Total business expenses (example) = $6,886.94 + $780 = $7,666.94.
- Net business profit example = $11,200 − $7,666.94 ≈ $3,533.06.
- Self-employment tax calculation (approx): Net earnings for SE tax = 92.35% × net profit. SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (0.9235 × net profit). Using the example net profit of $3,533.06 gives roughly $499 of SE tax. Half of SE tax (~$250) is an adjustment on Schedule 1.

Simplified home-office option
- There’s a simplified method (a flat rate per square foot, e.g., $5/sq ft up to 300 sq ft). For 165 sq ft that would be 165 × $5 = $825. This is claimed directly on Schedule C and avoids Form 8829. Compare $825 (simplified) vs ~$6,887 (actual-expense). For your rent level, the actual-expense method will produce a much larger deduction — but only use actual expenses if you have records and don’t exceed allowed limits (and remember the deduction cannot exceed the business profit).

4) Charitable contributions
- $2,000 to St. Mary’s Food Bank is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you get no federal deduction for that $2,000. (You should keep receipts you mentioned.)

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($780)
- If those expenses relate to the self‑employment (graphic design) — e.g., supplies, software, travel for clients — they are deductible on Schedule C and were treated above.
- If those are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W-2 job, they are not deductible for federal tax under current law (TCJA suspension through 2025).

6) Student loan interest vs. other adjustments
- Student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) and half of your SE tax (approx $250 in the example) are reported on Schedule 1 and reduce your AGI.

7) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- Based on the numbers you gave, itemized deductions are very likely to be lower than the 2024 MFJ standard deduction ($29,200). To justify itemizing you’d need substantially more deductible items (mortgage interest, large state & local taxes up to the SALT cap, large medical expenses above threshold, larger charitable gifts, etc.). So I expect you should take the standard deduction for federal purposes.

8) State (California) notes
- You’ll need to file a California return (Form 540). California generally starts with federal AGI and has its own adjustments — charitable contributions and state tax rules differ from federal in some areas. If you paid CA state income tax or had significant state/local taxes, remember SALT cap ($10,000) limits federal deduction but not necessarily state calculations.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main form)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax go here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your $11,200 and business expenses (home office portion if using actual expenses, plus any business supplies)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax) — calculate SE tax and half deduction
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual-expense method for the home office — this computes the allowable home-office deduction that flows to Schedule C. If you use the simplified method, you do not file Form 8829; you report the simplified deduction directly on Schedule C.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (doesn’t look likely based on numbers shown)
- Form 1040 instructions and worksheets as needed

Other possible forms
- Form 4562 — generally used for depreciation; if you own the home and claim depreciation for the business use of your home, you’d use Form 4562. Because you rent, you generally won’t be depreciating the residence.
- California Form 540 (state return) and associated CA schedules.

