Thanks — I can walk you through what to report, what’s deductible, whether to itemize, how to handle the home office, and which federal (and a note on state) forms you’ll likely need. A couple of quick clarifying questions before I run any numbers for you:
- Is the $780 in “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the graphic‑design work, or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job? (This changes how they’re treated.)
- Is the $2,700 the interest you actually paid in the 2024 tax year and do you have a Form 1098‑E from the lender?

What you must report
- W‑2 wages from Riverside Hospital: report on Form 1040 (you’ll include the W‑2 info).
- Graphic‑design income ($11,200): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). That’s self‑employment income.
- Interest: Citi savings ($1,780) + E*TRADE ($195) = $1,975 total interest income — report on Form 1040 and, because total interest income > $1,500, you’ll also complete Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).

Self‑employment / business items
- Schedule C: report the $11,200 gross receipts and any ordinary and necessary business expenses (e.g., supplies, the business portion of your home office, business mileage, software, etc.).
- Schedule SE: you’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings if net profit > $400. The SE tax is calculated on Schedule SE; one-half of the SE tax is an ABOVE‑THE‑LINE deduction on Form 1040.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be able to take up to 20% of qualified business income (Form 8995 or 8995‑A), but eligibility and the amount depend on taxable income and whether the business is treated as a specified service. If you want, I can check this once we have your full income numbers and confirm whether graphic design qualifies in your situation.

Home‑office deduction
You can choose either the simplified method or the actual expenses method (but not both for the same year):
- Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 165 sq ft that’s 165 × $5 = $825.
- Actual expenses method: you allocate actual home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, repairs, etc.) by the business percentage of your home. Using the numbers you gave:
  - Home area: 165 sq ft exclusive office / 690 sq ft total = 165/690 ≈ 23.91% business use.
  - Annual rent: $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
  - Business portion of rent ≈ 28,800 × 23.91% ≈ $6,890–$6,900.
  - If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, or other shared home costs, you would apply the same percentage to those to get the business portion.
- Which method to pick: based on the numbers you gave, the actual‑expense method (mostly because of the rent amount) will likely produce a larger deduction than the simplified method. Note: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from the business (there is a loss limitation). Also, if you use the actual method you generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home); simplified method does not use Form 8829.

Student loan interest
- The student‑loan interest deduction is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1 / Form 1040 adjustments) if you qualify. There are income phaseouts and a maximum allowable yearly deduction (you’ll want your Form 1098‑E from the lender). If your MAGI is within the allowable range, some or all of the $2,700 you report as interest paid may be deductible up to the statutory cap. I recommend verifying the 1098‑E and confirming the 2024 phaseout rules (I can help estimate once you confirm your AGI and whether the $2,700 is 2024 interest).

Charitable contribution
- Your $2,000 cash gift to St. Mary’s Food Bank is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt. Cash gifts are normally deductible as charitable contributions if you itemize.

Unreimbursed employee expenses
- If the $780 are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, those are no longer deductible on the federal return (suspended by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act through 2025). If they are business expenses for your graphic‑design work, report them on Schedule C.

Itemize vs standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly)
- For most taxpayers filing jointly, the standard deduction is substantially larger than a small set of itemized deductions (charity $2,000 only here, plus any state/local taxes and mortgage interest if you have them).
- Based on what you provided (charitable $2,000 and no mortgage interest or large medical expenses listed), you’ll most likely be better off taking the standard deduction rather than itemizing. The home‑office deduction is a business expense (Schedule C) and is independent of itemizing versus standard deduction — it does not go on Schedule A.
- If you have additional itemizable items (state income taxes or property taxes you paid, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.), list them and I’ll re-check.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like student‑loan interest and other adjustments
- Schedule B — if interest/dividend income > $1,500 (applies here)
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (graphic design)
- Schedule SE — Self‑Employment Tax (if net self‑employment earnings > $400)
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if using the actual‑expense home‑office method)
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — Qualified Business Income deduction (if applicable)
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (charitable contributions, state taxes, mortgage interest, etc.)
- Keep documentation: W‑2(s), 1099‑NEC or 1099‑K (if any) for self‑employment income, 1098‑E (student loan interest), 1099‑INT (interest), receipts for charitable gifts, and records for home‑office expenses and business expenses.

State (California) note
- California has its own return (Form 540) and some differences in what is allowed. For example, state rules on student loan interest or itemized deductions can differ. If you’d like, I can summarize expected CA differences or list forms you’ll need for CA.

Other important items
- Estimated tax payments: because you have self‑employment income, you may owe self‑employment tax plus income tax on that income. If you expect to owe more than you had withheld, consider quarterly estimated tax payments to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Records: keep good documentation for home‑office allocation, business receipts, and charitable receipts.
- Limits: some deductions have income phaseouts or caps; for example, the student‑loan interest deduction has income limits and a statutory maximum. QBI has income thresholds and special rules for “specified service” businesses.

If you want, I can:
