- Home office: 165 sq ft exclusive use; home = 690 sq ft => business-use percentage = 165/690 ≈ 23.91%

2) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- For Married Filing Jointly (MFJ) the standard deduction is substantially larger than $2,000 in recent years. With only $2,000 of charitable gifts listed and no mortgage interest, large property tax, or other itemizable expenses shown, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction rather than itemizing.
- Action: Unless you have large additional itemizable items (mortgage interest, large unreimbursed medical expenses above the threshold, property taxes/state income taxes up to the $10k SALT cap, or larger charitable gifts), take the standard deduction.

3) Student loan interest ($2,700)
- Federal rule: the maximum student-loan interest deduction is $2,500 (above-the-line adjustment to income), but it phases out at higher MAGI levels. If your MAGI is below the phaseout threshold you can deduct up to $2,500; the extra $200 would not be deductible.
- Important: The deduction applies to interest paid during the tax year (you’ll normally get Form 1098-E from the lender if > $600). Confirm that the $2,700 is interest paid in tax year 2024. If it’s for earlier years, it does not apply to the 2024 return.
- Also note: Some state returns (including CA) may treat student loan interest differently — check California rules or consult a tax pro.

4) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($780)
- Under current federal law (Tax Cuts and Jobs Act through 2025), miscellaneous unreimbursed employee expenses (including many employee business expenses) are not deductible for most employees.
- Therefore your $780 of unreimbursed employee expenses is not deductible on your federal return unless you fall into a very narrow exception (e.g., certain reservists, qualified performing artists, etc.). California may differ for state return — check CA rules.

5) Home office (you’re self-employed with graphic design income) — how to treat and which method to use
- You may take a home office deduction because you have self-employment income and an exclusive-use area used regularly and exclusively for business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 165 sq ft → 165 * $5 = $825.
  b) Actual-expense method: allocate the business percentage of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.). Using only rent (28,800/year), your allocation is 23.91% → 28,800 * 0.23913 ≈ $6,888.
- Which to pick? The actual-expense method gives a much larger deduction here (≈ $6,888) compared to $825. If you have no other home-related expenses to add, the rent allocation alone already favors the actual method.
- Important limit: The home office deduction cannot exceed the gross profit from the business. If your Schedule C business has net profit less than the home-office portion, excess may be carried forward (see Form 8829 rules). With your stated numbers (11,200 gross), using the actual method yields net profit = 11,200 − 6,888 = ≈ 4,312 — so the deduction fits under your business income in this example.
- Reporting:
  - Actual-expense method: use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach to Schedule C.
  - Simplified method: claim directly on Schedule C (no Form 8829).
- Keep records: floor-plan, square footage calculations, lease/rent statements, utilities, and any other home-related bills.

6) Self-employment tax and related items
