  - SE tax ≈ 3,986 * 15.3% ≈ $610 (approx). Half of that (~$305) is an above-the-line deduction on Schedule 1.
- The SE tax is in addition to income tax; your W-2 withholding may cover some of your total tax — consider estimated tax payments if you expect to owe significant tax.

7) Interest & dividends
- Report taxable interest (Citi $1,780) and E*TRADE $195 (likely dividends/interest) on the federal return.
- If total taxable interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 for the year, you must file Schedule B. With $1,975 total here, you’d include Schedule B.

8) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and half of self-employment tax deduction flow here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $11,200 and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you choose the actual-expense method for the home office
- Schedule B — if total interest/dividends > $1,500 (it is, based on figures you gave)
