9) Documentation to keep
- Receipts for charitable donations (you said you have them) — required for cash gifts; keep bank/credit card records and acknowledgment if > $250
- 1098-E from the loan servicer for student loan interest paid (if applicable for 2024)
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC from clients (if issued) or records of payments you received
- Bank/broker 1099-INT / 1099-DIV (Citi, E*TRADE)
- Lease/rent receipts, utility bills, and any documents proving exclusive use and square footage for home office
- Records of unreimbursed employee expenses (even if nondeductible federally, you may need for state or employer reimbursement records)

10) Practical recommendations / next steps
- Likely take the standard deduction for MFJ unless you have additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large property taxes up to $10k SALT cap, big charitable gifts, etc.).
- Use the actual home-office method (Form 8829) given the large rent portion — it appears to give a bigger deduction than the simplified method.
- Expect to file Schedule C and Schedule SE; that triggers self-employment tax and the deduction for half of SE tax.
- Confirm that the student loan interest amount ($2,700) is interest paid in 2024. If so, federal deduction is capped at $2,500 (subject to phaseout). Verify whether CA allows similar treatment.
- Confirm whether the $11,200 is gross receipts and whether you have additional business expenses (software, equipment, supplies, mileage, etc.) — those reduce net self-employment income and SE tax.
