Assuming the amounts you listed are for tax year 2024 and that the $780 is a business expense for the self-employment income, here’s how things generally work and a numeric example.

1) How each income item is reported
- W-2 wages ($79,400): Report on Form 1040 (from the W-2).
- Self-employment income ($11,200 from 2 clients): Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you received any 1099-NEC forms, include them; even if you did not receive 1099s you still must report the income.
- Interest from Citi ($1,780) and E*TRADE ($195): Report on Form 1040. If your total taxable interest and ordinary dividends exceed $1,500, you must attach Schedule B — your total interest here is $1,975, so you will need Schedule B.

2) Student loan interest
- The maximum student loan interest deduction is generally $2,500 (above-the-line adjustment on Schedule 1) and is subject to income phaseouts. With the income levels in your situation, you would likely be eligible for the full $2,500 (but I can confirm if you want me to compute MAGI and phaseout precisely).
- If you actually paid $2,700 in 2024, the deductible amount will be capped at $2,500.

3) Home-office deduction (self-employed only)
- Because you have self-employment income, you can claim the home-office deduction for the portion of your home used exclusively and regularly for business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft (limit 300 sq ft). For 165 sq ft → deduction = 165 * $5 = $825.
  b) Actual (regular) method: allocates actual home expenses (rent, utilities, insurance, repairs, etc.) by business-use percentage. Your business-use percentage = 165 / 690 ≈ 23.91%. Annual rent = $2,400 * 12 = $28,800. Rent portion attributable to the office ≈ $28,800 * 23.91% ≈ $6,888 (plus any allocable utilities, insurance, etc.). You would use Form 8829 to calculate & report this (or report on Schedule C with the proper entries).
- Because the rent portion alone (≈ $6,888) is much larger than the simplified amount ($825), the actual method will almost certainly give you a bigger deduction — assuming you have no other reason to prefer the simplified method (simplified is easier, no Form 8829 required).
- Note: the home-office deduction applies only to your self-employment business. If all of the office use is as an employee for your W-2 job (and not for the graphic-design business), it’s not deductible under current law.

4) Self-employment tax
- Your net self-employment profit (Schedule C) is subject to self-employment tax (~15.3%) on ~92.35% of net earnings. Half of the self-employment tax is an above-the-line deduction on Schedule 1.
- Home-office and business expenses reduce your Schedule C net profit and thus reduce your self-employment tax.

5) Unreimbursed employee expenses
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for a W-2 job are generally NOT deductible for tax years through 2025 under current law (TCJA). If the $780 is for your self-employment work, those are deductible on Schedule C.

6) Charitable contribution ($2,000)
- Cash contributions to qualified charities are deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. There is no separate “above-the-line” charitable deduction for most taxpayers in 2024. If you take the standard deduction, this $2,000 will not reduce taxable income.

7) Itemize vs. standard deduction (recommendation)
- 2024 standard deduction (married filing jointly) = $29,200.
- Likely itemizable items you listed: charitable $2,000, possibly state/local taxes paid (SALT limit $10,000), mortgage interest (if any). You did not list mortgage interest or state tax amounts — with only $2,000 of charitable donations and no mortgage interest listed, your itemized total is likely well below the $29,200 standard deduction.
- Conclusion (based on the data you gave): Take the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable deductions (large mortgage interest, large state taxes, large medical expenses over 7.5% of AGI, etc.). If you do have large additional itemizable deductions, re-run the comparison with those numbers.

8) Forms you’ll likely need
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income): for student loan interest (and for the deduction of half self-employment tax)
- Schedule B: if interest/dividends > $1,500 (you need this — total interest is $1,975)
- Schedule C: to report the graphic-design income and business expenses
- Form 8829: if you use the actual method for the home-office deduction (if you use the simplified method, Form 8829 is not used)
- Schedule SE: to compute self-employment tax
- Schedule A: only if you itemize (probably not, based on provided items)
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC / 1099-INT / 1099-DIV / 1099-B as applicable from payers and brokers — keep these handy and reconcile to what you report
- If you have federal estimated tax payments, those are reported on Form 1040 as well (payments already made).

9) Quick numeric example (illustrative; I used your numbers and assumed the $780 is a business expense and the $2,700 student interest was paid in 2024 and capped at $2,500)
- Income: W-2 $79,400 + self-employment gross $11,200 + interest $1,975 = $92,575 total.
- Option A — Actual home-office method (rent allocation ~ $6,888) plus $780 business expenses:
  - Schedule C net profit ≈ 11,200 − 6,888 − 780 = $3,532.
  - Self-employment tax (approx) on 92.35% of $3,532 ≈ $499; half deductible ≈ $250.
  - Student loan interest deduction capped at $2,500.
  - AGI ≈ (79,400 + 3,532 + 1,975) − (2,500 + 250) ≈ $82,158.
  - Taxable income after standard deduction (MFJ $29,200) ≈ $52,958.
- Option B — Simplified home-office ($825) + $780 expenses:
  - Schedule C net ≈ 11,200 − 825 − 780 = $9,595.
  - Self-employment tax on 92.35% of $9,595 ≈ $1,357; half deductible ≈ $679.
  - AGI ≈ (79,400 + 9,595 + 1,975) − (2,500 + 679) ≈ $87,792.
  - Taxable income after standard deduction ≈ $58,592.
- So in this example the actual method lowers taxable income by several thousand and reduces self-employment tax — the actual method appears better for you given the high share of rent attributable to the office.

10) Other important points
- If the $2,700 student loan interest was not paid in 2024, it is not deductible on your 2024 return — only interest paid in the tax year is deductible.
- The short broker amount ($195) could be interest, ordinary dividends, or short-term capital gains — confirm the 1099 you received from E*TRADE and include that correctly (e.g., 1099-INT vs 1099-DIV vs 1099-B).
- Keep documentation: receipts for business expenses, charity receipts, rent records, square footage calculation and proof of exclusive use for the home office.
- Because you have self-employment income, you may owe quarterly estimated taxes (or have underpayment penalties) — check if your withholding from the W-2 is sufficient after factoring business income and SE tax.

11) Next steps I can help with
- If you confirm (a) that the student loan interest was paid in 2024, (b) whether the $780 is business-related, and (c) whether you have mortgage interest or state tax paid for 2024, I’ll recompute estimates and finalize recommended forms/entries.
- I can also draft a checklist of documents to gather for a preparer or prepare an itemized worksheet you could use to decide standard vs itemize.

