If you want, answer those quickly and I’ll refine the numbers. Below is a clear, actionable summary and rough calculations based on the facts you gave.

1) What income you’ll report (federal)
- W‑2 wages: $79,400 (Riverside Hospital) — reported on Form 1040, wages line (with W‑2 attached).  
- Self‑employment income: $11,200 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).  
- Interest/dividends: $1,780 (Citi savings) + $195 (E*TRADE) = $1,975 — report on Form 1040; because total interest > $1,500, you’ll also file Schedule B. Expect 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV statements from those institutions.  

2) Self‑employment tax and business deductions
- Self‑employment income is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security & Medicare). You calculate net profit on Schedule C, then compute SE tax on Schedule SE. You can deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).  
- Any true business expenses for the graphic‑design work (software, supplies, advertising, part of phone bills used for business, etc.) belong on Schedule C and reduce net self‑employment income. If the $780 is business-related, include it on Schedule C.

3) Home‑office deduction (you stated 165 sq ft exclusive; home 690 sq ft; rent $2,400/month)
You can choose between the simplified method or the actual‑expense method. The usage must be regular, exclusive, and for business (you said “exclusive” — that’s good).

- Percentage of home used for business = 165 / 690 ≈ 23.91%.

Simplified method:
- Deduction = $5.00 × 165 sq ft = $825 (no Form 8829; report on Schedule C).

Actual‑expense method (rent example only; utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs could also be allocated if you have records):
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.  
- Home‑office share of rent = 28,800 × 23.91% ≈ $6,880 (this is deducted on Schedule C as a business expense).  
- You can also allocate a share of utilities, internet (business portion), repairs, etc., which increases the deduction if present. If you own the home rather than rent, depreciation enters into the calculation (Form 8829).

Important limits/notes:
- The home‑office deduction is limited to the gross income from that business (deductions can’t create an ordinary loss from the business for the year — excess home‑office expense may be carried forward). With $11,200 gross, a $6,880 deduction is allowed; it does not exceed the business income.  
- If you use the actual method for specific expenses, you generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home). If you use the simplified method, you do NOT file Form 8829.

Which method likely makes more sense here?
- Based only on rent, the actual method (≈ $6,880) is much larger than the simplified $825, so actual method likely yields a larger tax benefit. If you have utilities/internet that you can allocate, that further favors actual. Use actual method if you want the bigger deduction and you have documentation; use simplified for simplicity. I can walk through exact Schedule C/8829 entries if you want.

4) Student‑loan interest
- Maximum federal student‑loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 (an above‑the‑line adjustment), but it phases out at higher MAGI. Given the incomes you listed (W‑2 + interest + self‑employment), you are likely well under common phaseout thresholds — so you can probably claim up to $2,500 (even though you paid $2,700). You’ll need Form 1098‑E (or lender statement) showing interest paid in the tax year. If the payments you referenced were for earlier years (2019–2020) that doesn’t count — the deduction only covers interest actually paid in 2024.

5) Charitable giving
- $2,000 to St. Mary’s Food Bank is deductible if it’s to a qualified charity and you have a receipt. But you only get the benefit if you itemize on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction, the cash gift gives no separate federal tax benefit (with typical returns).

6) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($780)
- For federal return: unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible for most taxpayers (suspended through tax year 2025 under current federal rules). If those $780 are business expenses for your self‑employment, include them on Schedule C instead. If they are tied to your W‑2 job (and not reimbursed), they generally are NOT deductible on your federal return.

7) Itemize vs standard deduction (married filing jointly)
- Standard deduction for 2024 (Married Filing Jointly) is $29,200.  
- Your one clear itemized amount shown is $2,000 charitable (plus any state income or real‑estate taxes you paid). The SALT limit (state & local taxes deduction) is limited to $10,000 total. Unless you have large mortgage interest, property taxes, or significant state tax payments, your itemized total is very likely far less than the $29,200 standard deduction. Therefore, for federal filing you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction — unless you have other large itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, big state income or property taxes, medical expenses above threshold, etc.). Note: the home office deduction does NOT affect the standard vs itemized decision directly — it reduces your business income (Schedule C) and thus AGI.

8) Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)  
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments like student‑loan interest and half of SE tax (some lines have moved on the 1040 but Schedule 1 is commonly used)  
