Thanks — this is enough to give clear, practical guidance. I’ll first summarize what you can (likely) deduct and what tax forms you’ll need, then show two example calculations (regular home-office method vs. the simplified method) so you can see which is better for your situation. A few short questions are at the end — answering them will let me refine numbers and any phaseout rules that might apply.

High-level summary
- File Form 1040 for 2024 (Married Filing Jointly).
- Report wages from Riverside Hospital on Form 1040 (W-2).
- Report freelance graphic-design income (the $11,200) on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
- Pay/self-report self-employment tax on Schedule SE (and take half of that as an adjustment on Schedule 1).
- Report interest income (Citi savings $1,780; plus E*TRADE $195 if that is interest/dividends) on Schedule B if total interest and ordinary dividends > $1,500. Brokers/banks should issue Form 1099-INT or 1099-DIV.
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 is an above-the-line deduction (reported on Schedule 1) if you meet the income limits and the amount was paid in the tax year. You mentioned $2,700 paid — the maximum federal deduction is $2,500.
- Charitable cash gift $2,000 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- Unreimbursed expenses: if these $780 are business expenses related to your self-employment, deduct them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for W-2 employment, they are not deductible on your federal return under current law (through at least 2025).
- Home office (165 sq ft exclusive; home 690 sq ft, rent $2,400/month): you can claim either the simplified method or the regular (actual expenses) method for your Schedule C home-office deduction. Based on the numbers you gave, the regular method (allocating a portion of rent) will almost certainly give a larger deduction. See calculations below.
- State return (California): you’ll file CA Form 540. State rules differ in details (conformity for student loan interest, some adjustments), so check CA rules or confirm with a tax pro for state-specific differences.
- Keep receipts and documentation: charitable receipts, student loan interest statement (Form 1098-E) showing interest paid in 2024, records of business income and expenses, rent receipts/lease, and method used to compute home-office area.

Which deduction method (standard vs. itemize)?
- 2024 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly) is $29,200. With only a $2,000 charitable contribution and no mortgage interest or large state/local taxes listed, the standard deduction will very likely be larger than your itemized deductions. So you will probably take the standard deduction unless you have other itemizable items (mortgage interest, large SALT up to the $10k cap, medical expenses exceeding the threshold, etc.).

Home-office — simplified vs. regular method (short explanation)
- Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. Your 165 sq ft -> $825 deduction. Easy, no Form 8829.
- Regular method: allocate actual home expenses by the business-use percentage. Your business-use % = 165 / 690 = 23.91%. Rent: $2,400/month = $28,800/year. Home-office share of rent = 23.91% × $28,800 ≈ $6,884.80. You report the home-office deduction (and other allowable home office-related expenses) on Form 8829 and carry to Schedule C.
- Regular method will yield a much bigger deduction here because rent is large. The regular-method home-office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (and thus also reduces self-employment tax). Note: the home-office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from that business (before the home-office deduction); excess may be carried forward.

Forms you will likely use
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and the deductible half of SE tax
- Schedule B — if total interest and ordinary dividends > $1,500 (your Citi interest + E*TRADE likely exceed this)
- Schedule C — report self-employment income and business expenses (including home-office via line 30 or via amount from Form 8829)
- Form 8829 — if you elect the regular method for home office (calculates allowable share of rent, utilities, etc.)
- Schedule SE — self-employment tax (also drives the deductible half of SE tax)
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (doesn’t look likely with the info given)
- CA Form 540 — California state return (plus CA schedules as required)

Example numbers — two scenarios (to illustrate the impact)
Assumptions used:
- Wages = $79,400
- Self-employment gross = $11,200
- Interest = $1,780 (Citi) + $195 (E*TRADE) = $1,975 (I treat the $195 as interest/ordinary income here; if it’s capital gains/dividends the tax treatment differs slightly but it still flows to 1040)
- Charitable = $2,000
- Student loan interest actually paid in 2024 = $2,700 (federal max deduction = $2,500)
- Unreimbursed expenses treated as business expenses (if they are employee unreimbursed, they are NOT deductible)

A) Regular home-office method (allocate rent)
- Business-use % = 165 / 690 = 23.91%
- Home-office portion of rent = 23.91% × $28,800 ≈ $6,884.80
- Business expenses: home-office $6,884.80 + other business expenses $780 = $7,664.80
- Schedule C net profit = $11,200 − $7,664.80 ≈ $3,535.20
- Net earnings for SE tax = $3,535.20 × 0.9235 ≈ $3,266
- Schedule SE tax (15.3% of net earnings) ≈ $500
- Deductible half of SE tax (Schedule 1 adjustment) ≈ $250
- Student loan interest deduction (if eligible) = $2,500 (max)
- Above-the-line adjustments ≈ $2,500 + $250 = $2,750
- Total income before adjustments = 79,400 + 1,975 + 3,535 ≈ $84,910
- AGI after adjustments ≈ $84,910 − $2,750 = $82,160
- Subtract standard deduction $29,200 → taxable income ≈ $52,960

B) Simplified home-office method ($5/sq ft)
- Home-office deduction = 165 × $5 = $825
- Business expenses: $825 + $780 = $1,605
- Schedule C net profit = $11,200 − $1,605 = $9,595
- Net earnings for SE tax = $9,595 × 0.9235 ≈ $8,861
- Schedule SE tax ≈ $1,355
- Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $678
