Thanks — this is enough to give a clear, practical answer and next steps. I’ll summarize what’s deductible, run through a quick numeric example to show whether you should itemize or take the standard deduction, explain how to treat the home office, list the federal (and California) forms you’ll likely need, and finish with a short checklist and a couple of clarifying questions.

Short summary / recommendation
- Based on the numbers you gave, you will almost certainly take the federal standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly). Your itemizable deductions shown (charity $2,000) are far below the 2024 MFJ standard deduction, and the home-office deduction is a business expense for your self-employment income (not an itemized deduction).
- Claim the self-employment income and home-office deduction on Schedule C (and Form 8829 if you use the actual-expense method). You’ll also need Schedule SE for self-employment tax and Schedule B for interest.
- The student loan interest may be deductible (up to $2,500) as an adjustment to income if you meet the MAGI limits — from the rough AGI calculation below, you appear eligible, but I’ll ask one clarification about the loan year below.

What you can deduct (federal)
- W-2 unreimbursed employee expenses ($780): Generally not deductible on your federal return for tax years since 2018 (miscellaneous unreimbursed employee expenses are suspended). If those were business expenses related to your self-employment work, they should be reported on Schedule C instead.
- Self-employment (graphic design) business expenses: Deductible on Schedule C. That includes either:
  - Actual-expense method for home office (see below), or
  - Simplified home-office method (up to $5 per sq ft, max 300 sq ft).
- Home office: deductible if you meet the “exclusive and regular use” test and the office is your principal place of business (you said 165 sq ft exclusive — that meets the exclusive-use test). See details below.
- Interest income ($1,780 Citi + $195 E*TRADE = $1,975): Taxable as interest. Because your total taxable interest is over $1,500, you will likely need Schedule B.
- Charitable gift ($2,000 to St. Mary’s, receipt available): Deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). Because it’s small relative to the standard deduction for MFJ, it likely won’t change the decision to itemize.
- Student loan interest ($2,700 reported): Up to $2,500 can be claimed as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1) for qualifying payments in the tax year, subject to MAGI phaseouts for MFJ. (See clarifying question below.)
- State & local taxes (SALT), mortgage interest, property taxes: you did not list these. SALT is capped at $10,000 federally; if you have state income tax or property taxes withheld/paid that could factor into itemizing, but you didn’t list other amounts, so likely still below the standard deduction.

Home-office treatment (two methods)
1) Actual-expense method (you allocate part of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc.)
- Office percentage = 165 sq ft / 690 sq ft = 165/690 ≈ 23.913% (≈23.9%).
- Rent: $2,400/month × 12 = $28,800/year. Rent portion attributable to office ≈ 23.913% × $28,800 ≈ $6,887.
- If you use actual method you may also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. If you used actual method you generally prepare Form 8829 for business use of the home (reported with Schedule C).
- The allowable home-office deduction is limited by the net profit from the business — it cannot create a business loss that offsets other income in some situations (Form 8829 handles carryforward if limited).

2) Simplified method
- $5 per sq ft × 165 sq ft = $825 (simpler, no Form 8829 needed). This is often used by taxpayers with small/home-based side gigs; in your case the actual method (rent portion) produces a larger deduction ($6,887) and so is likely more favorable if you can document shared home expenses.

Effect on self-employment tax and AGI (rough estimate)
(These are approximate; I show them so you can compare simplified vs actual home-office method.)

A. Using the actual home-office allocation (rent portion ≈ $6,887)
- Self-employment gross: $11,200
- Business expenses (home office rent portion): ≈ $6,887
- Net Schedule C profit ≈ $4,313
- Self-employment tax (approx): SE tax ≈ 15.3% × (0.9235 × 4,313) ≈ $610 (approx)
- Deductible half of SE tax (above-the-line) ≈ $305
