- SE tax and half SE tax are larger (SE tax roughly $1,466; half ≈ $733)
- AGI after half SE (and student loan interest) will be higher, so taxable income will be larger compared with actual method.
Conclusion: Using the actual allocation (if properly documented and you have eligible share of utilities etc.) will almost certainly lower taxable income more than the simplified method.

Student loan interest specifics
- The student loan interest deduction covers interest paid in the tax year and requires that the interest was paid on a qualified student loan; the lender typically issues Form 1098‑E.
- You listed $2,700 (Cornerstone, 2019–2020) — please clarify whether that $2,700 is interest you paid in tax year 2024. If it is interest paid in 2019–2020, you cannot deduct it in 2024. If it’s interest paid in 2024 and you have (or can get) Form 1098‑E, you can potentially deduct up to $2,500, subject to MAGI phaseouts for MFJ. Based on the AGI rough estimate above, you would be under the phaseout threshold and should be eligible for the full $2,500 (if payments were in 2024).

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments to income — student loan interest deduction goes here)
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — required if taxable interest > $1,500
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report graphic-design income and business expenses
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you choose the actual-expense home-office method (often used with Schedule C)
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
