Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer up front
- Most likely you should take the standard deduction for 2024 (it will almost certainly exceed the itemized deductible items you listed).
- You can (and should) report the $11,200 graphic-design income on Schedule C and claim a home‑office deduction for the self‑employment business. That deduction reduces your self‑employment tax and income tax.
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment (up to $2,500, subject to income limits) even if you take the standard deduction.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job are NOT deductible under current law (with rare exceptions).
- Forms you’ll likely need: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule 1, Schedule B, and either Form 8829 (actual home office method) or use the simplified home‑office calculation on Schedule C. You may also need Form 1040‑ES for estimated tax payments.

Detailed breakdown and next steps

1) Income to report
- W‑2 from Riverside Hospital: $79,400 — reported on Form 1040 (W‑2).
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $11,200 — report on Schedule C (Form 1040). Keep records/receipts for any business expenses.
- Interest: $1,780 (Citi) and $195 (E*TRADE) — report as taxable interest on Form 1040. Because total interest (1,780 + 195 = $1,975) exceeds $1,500 you’ll also complete Schedule B.

2) Home office (you are self‑employed for the graphic design work)
- You may claim a home‑office deduction because you have self‑employment income and the office is used exclusively and regularly for the business.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 165 sq ft: 165 × $5 = $825 (enter on Schedule C).
  b) Actual (regular) method: allocate actual home expenses by percentage of home used for business. You gave rent $2,400/month → $28,800/year. Business percentage = 165 / 690 ≈ 23.91%. Rent portion deductible = $28,800 × 0.2391 ≈ $6,888 (plus you could allocate utilities, insurance, renter’s insurance portion, repairs, etc.). To claim actual expenses you generally complete Form 8829 and attach it to Schedule C.
- Which method to use? The actual method here yields a much larger deduction because of high rent; however:
  - The home‑office deduction cannot exceed your gross business income from that business (before the home office deduction). If your business has other deductible expenses, the home office could create or increase a Schedule C loss — that’s generally allowed, but check rules for at‑risk/passive activity if other rules apply.
  - Simplified is easier (no Form 8829) but likely smaller in this case.
- The home‑office deduction reduces net Schedule C profit and therefore reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax.

3) Student loan interest
- You reported $2,700 paid to Cornerstone (loans from 2019–2020?). The maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (if you are eligible). You can claim up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (even if you take the standard deduction).
- Eligibility is subject to modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) phaseouts and you cannot claim the deduction if you are married filing separately. I can estimate eligibility if you tell me your spouse’s income and any other adjustments.

4) Charitable contributions
- $2,000 to St. Mary’s Food Bank — deductible only if you itemize. Since your likely itemized total is small (just $2,000 unless you have mortgage interest, high state/local taxes, or other deductible items), you’ll probably be better off taking the standard deduction and you will not be able to deduct this gift on your 1040. Keep the receipt in case you decide to itemize.

5) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($780)
- Under current tax law (post‑TCJA), unreimbursed employee business expenses for a W‑2 employee are not deductible for most taxpayers. If those $780 are actually business expenses for your self‑employment work, report them on Schedule C (they are deductible there). If they are for your W‑2 job, they generally are not deductible.

6) Itemize or standard deduction?
- The 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly is roughly $29k (check the exact IRS amount for the year when you file). Your only listed potential itemized deduction is $2,000 charitable contribution (unless you also have mortgage interest, sizable state taxes, medical expenses above the floor, etc.). That is far below the standard deduction, so you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.
- Note: Student loan interest (up to $2,500) is an above‑the‑line deduction you get even if you take the standard deduction.

7) Self‑employment tax and estimated payments
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare, about 15.3% on net self‑employment earnings), reported and calculated on Schedule SE. You may owe self‑employment tax in addition to income tax.
- You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income.
- If you expect to owe tax beyond withholding, you should pay quarterly estimated taxes using Form 1040‑ES to avoid penalties.

8) Other items to watch
- 1099s: Clients may issue 1099‑NEC or 1099‑K for payments; you must report all income whether or not you receive a form.
- QBI (Qualified Business Income) deduction: With self‑employment income you may be eligible for a 20% deduction of qualified business income (Form 8995 or 8995‑A) — eligibility depends on taxable income thresholds. I can check that if you provide spouse income and any other deductions.
- Keep receipts for charitable gifts, business expenses, rent payments, and student loan interest (Form 1098‑E if issued) in case of audit.

9) Forms you will likely use
- Form 1040
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report the $11,200 and business expenses
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual home‑expense method; otherwise use the simplified method on Schedule C
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments (student loan interest deduction, half SE tax deduction)
- Schedule B — for interest income (aggregate interest > $1,500)
- Form 1040‑ES — for estimated tax payments (if needed)
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — if claiming the QBI deduction (may be applicable)

What I recommend you do next
- Confirm/answer a few clarifying questions so I can give a precise estimate:
  1. Is your filing status definitely Married Filing Jointly and do you want me to assume spouse had no income? (If spouse has income, please give that amount.)
  2. Did you actually pay the $2,700 in student loan interest in the tax year you’re filing for? (Was a 1098‑E issued?)
  3. Are the $780 unreimbursed expenses for the self‑employment work or for your W‑2 employment?
