I’m not a CPA, but I can walk you through how the numbers and rules generally apply and what to check with a tax pro. I’ll (1) summarize which items are deductible and how to treat each, (2) show a numeric illustration of the home-office and self-employment effects, (3) say whether itemizing is likely beneficial vs taking the standard deduction, and (4) list the forms and records you’ll need.

Key facts you gave
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (San Diego, CA)
- W-2 (Riverside Hospital): $79,400
- Self‑employment (graphic design, 2 clients): $11,200
- Interest income: $1,780 (Citi savings)
- Brokerage income: $195 (E*TRADE)
- Rent: $2,400/month = $28,800/year for a 690 sq ft unit
- Home office: 165 sq ft used exclusively for business
- Student loan interest paid: $2,700 (Cornerstone, 2019–2020)
- Charitable cash gift: $2,000 (receipt)
- “Unreimbursed expenses” $780 — clarify whether these are employee unreimbursed W‑2 expenses or self‑employment business expenses

1) What is deductible (federal) — item-by-item
- W-2 wages: fully included in gross income; any tax withholding shown on W-2 reduces tax due.
- Self‑employment income: report on Schedule C. You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (supplies, software, business portion of internet, phone, travel, and the home‑office portion of rent/utilities if you use the actual expense method).
- Home office: deductible if you use the space exclusively and regularly for your trade/business. Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × square feet up to 300 sq ft → 165 × $5 = $825 (easy, no Form 8829).
  - Regular (actual expenses): prorate rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., by the business percentage (see below). If you rent, you may deduct the business portion of rent.
- Self‑employment tax: you pay SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net Schedule C profit; half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 is an above‑the‑line deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule 1), subject to income phaseouts. You reported $2,700 paid — the maximum deduction is $2,500 (if you qualify by MAGI). Phaseouts apply; with the income levels below, you’ll likely qualify for most or all, but confirm with exact MAGI rules or your CPA.
- Charitable contributions: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Cash donations to qualified charities are generally fully deductible (you have the receipt).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses: NOT deductible on federal Form 1040 for most employees (TCJA suspension 2018–2025). If the $780 are business expenses related to your self‑employment, include them on Schedule C instead.
- Interest/dividends from bank/brokerage: report as interest/dividends on Form 1040 (1099‑INT/1099‑DIV/1099‑B as applicable).

2) Home office math (specific to your numbers)
- Business percentage = home office sq ft / total sq ft = 165 / 690 = 0.23913 ≈ 23.9%
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800
- Rent portion allocable to home office (actual‑expense method) ≈ 28,800 × 23.913% ≈ $6,887
- Example additional allocable items (estimate; you’ll need receipts):
  - Internet portion: e.g., $720 × 23.9% ≈ $172
  - Renter’s insurance portion: e.g., $240 × 23.9% ≈ $57
- So, using actual expenses, home‑office portion could be roughly $6,887 + other prorated utilities/insurance/repairs ≈ ~$7,100 (example).
- Using simplified method: $825 (smaller, simpler).

Important limits for home office:
- If the home‑office deduction (when combined with other business deductions) reduces your Schedule C net profit to zero, the excess home‑office deduction may be limited and may carry forward. You can’t deduct more home‑office than allowable against that business income in some cases — check Form 8829 instructions or your tax advisor.
- If the home‑office deduction is legitimate and your Schedule C still shows net profit, it reduces both income tax and SE tax (because it reduces net profit used to compute SE tax).

3) Self‑employment tax & illustrative net profit
Two quick scenarios (approximate; round numbers):

A) If the $780 are self‑employment business expenses and you use actual home-office:
- Gross self‑employment: $11,200
- Home‑office actual expenses (example): ~$7,116
- Other business expenses: $780
- Net profit ≈ 11,200 − 7,116 − 780 = $3,304
- Self‑employment tax (approx): net × 92.35% × 15.3% ≈ 3,304 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ ~$467
- Half SE tax deduction ≈ $234 (above‑the‑line)
- Taxable self‑employment income (after half SE): net minus half SE deduction (affects 1040 AGI)

B) If the $780 are NOT business expenses (employee unreimbursed) and you use actual home-office:
- Net profit ≈ 11,200 − 7,116 = $4,084
- SE tax ≈ 4,084 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ ~$576
- Half SE ≈ $288

(If you use the simplified $825 home‑office deduction instead, net profit is larger, SE tax increases accordingly.)

4) Standard deduction vs itemize
- Student loan interest is an ABOVE‑THE‑LINE adjustment (affects AGI) — it does not require itemizing.
- Charitable $2,000 only helps if you itemize.
- You did not list mortgage interest, property tax, or a large SALT amount. For married filing jointly in 2024 the standard deduction is materially higher than in past years — with only $2,000 of charity listed, your likely itemized total (without big mortgage/property tax or state taxes) will be much less than the standard deduction.
- Conclusion: With the information you gave (charity $2,000 and no large mortgage interest or big property taxes listed), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction for federal tax. If you have significant other itemizable amounts (large state/local taxes up to the SALT cap of $10,000, mortgage interest, large medical expenses above the floor, etc.), re‑check with your CPA.

Note on California state tax:
- California conformity differs from federal in some respects. California generally requires you to start with federal AGI but has differences in what is deductible. For example, some federal suspensions remain disallowed or adjusted at state level. Check CA Form 540 guidance or ask your tax preparer about state treatment of student loan interest and unreimbursed employee expenses. I recommend confirming state specifics with a CA tax professional.

5) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return
- Schedule 1 — adjustments to income (student loan interest, half of self‑employment tax is an adjustment reported elsewhere)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $11,200 and all business expenses
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use actual expense method for home office). If you use simplified method, you do not file Form 8829; report the simplified deduction directly on Schedule C.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (unlikely based on info provided). Charitable contributions would appear here.
- Form 8995 / 8995‑A — if you have qualified business income (QBI) deduction; you may be eligible for QBI (20% of qualified business income) on pass-through/self-employed income — depends on W‑2 wage limitation, taxable income thresholds and if the activity qualifies. With a small net profit, you may get some QBI deduction; this is worth checking with your preparer.
- Federal 1099s/1098s you should receive and keep: W‑2 (hospital), 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC (if clients issued), 1099‑INT (bank interest), 1099‑DIV/1099‑B (brokerage), 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) or proof of payments if no 1098‑E.

Records to keep
- W‑2 and 1099s
- Bank/brokerage 1099s
- Receipts/invoices for graphic design expenses (software, supplies, client costs)
- Lease and rent receipts (proof of rent paid), a simple floor plan or note verifying 165 sq ft exclusive business use
- Receipts for internet, renter’s insurance, utilities, repairs that you allocate to business
- Student loan interest statement or lender statement (Form 1098‑E if available)
- Charity receipt for $2,000 (donation acknowledgment)
- Mileage logs if you use vehicle for business

6) Other considerations and recommended actions
- Confirm whether the $780 are self‑employment business expenses. If they are, include them on Schedule C; if they are unreimbursed W‑2 expenses, they’re not deductible on federal return.
- Decide simplified vs actual home‑office method. Actual method appears to produce a much larger deduction for you (because rent is high compared to overall square footage), but it requires Form 8829 and documentation. The simplified method is easy ($825).
- Check student loan interest phaseout for married filing jointly for 2024 using IRS tables; with your combined income (~$79,400 + self‑employment + interest ≈ $92k gross before deductions), you will most likely be under the phaseout and eligible for the full $2,500 (but confirm).
- You may need to make estimated tax payments or adjust withholding to cover income tax + self‑employment tax. With a small Schedule C net, SE tax is modest, but check withholding levels on W‑2.
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income on your Schedule C, subject to rules. With a modest net, QBI could reduce taxable income — ask your preparer about it.

7) Would you like me to do any of the following?
- Draft a concise summary email to your accountant with these numbers and ask them to prepare/confirm (I can draft and/or send if you want).
- Run a more detailed, line-by-line worksheet with alternative scenarios (actual vs simplified home office) if you provide your annual utility/insurance numbers and confirm whether the $780 is business related.
