What you can (likely) deduct
- Self-employment business expenses (Schedule C)
  - If the $780 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for your graphic design work, they are deductible on Schedule C. (If they are unreimbursed employee expenses tied to your W-2 job, they are NOT deductible for federal returns post-2017.)
  - Home office deduction (see options below) — only available for the self‑employed portion of your income (your graphic design work).
  - Other ordinary and necessary business expenses (software, supplies, advertising, etc.) are deductible on Schedule C if they relate to the design business.
- Home office deduction (only for the self-employed business). Two options:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 165 sq ft that’s 165 × $5 = $825 (deducted on Schedule C; no Form 8829).
  - Actual-expense method: deduct the business-use percentage of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.). Business percentage = 165 / 690 ≈ 23.91%. If you only have rent shown, that would be ~23.91% × $28,800 = $6,888 of rent allocable to the business (plus any utilities portion if you include them). To use actual-expense method you should complete Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C.
  - Important: the home office must be used regularly and exclusively for business. The deduction cannot exceed gross income from that business (there are special loss/carryover rules for excess).
- Self-employment tax and related deduction
  - You must pay self‑employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE). You may deduct one-half of your SE tax as an adjustment on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- Student loan interest
  - Up to $2,500 of student loan interest may be deductible as an above-the-line adjustment (Schedule 1) — the maximum is $2,500 (so your $2,700 payment would be limited to $2,500). There are income phase-outs for married filing jointly; with your incomes shown you’re likely under the phaseout threshold but I can check if you want a precise MAGI calculation.
- Charitable contribution
  - $2,000 cash gift is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). There is no separate above-the-line charitable deduction for 2024 (the temporary above-the-line rules expired).
- Interest income
  - Report the $1,975 interest on Form 1040. Because total taxable interest is over $1,500, you’ll also file Schedule B.

Unallowed (or limited) deductions
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job are not deductible on the federal return (suspended by TCJA). If the $780 is for the design business, it is deductible on Schedule C; if it’s for your hospital job, it is not deductible.
- The student loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 (not the full $2,700 you paid).
- SALT (state & local tax) deduction is limited to $10,000 (if you do itemize). You didn’t list state/local taxes or property tax, so itemized deductions may be small.

Itemize vs. standard deduction (federal)
- 2024 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is $29,200.
- Your likely itemizable deductions (from the data you gave) are only the $2,000 charitable contribution and whatever state taxes you actually paid (you didn’t list them). Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment (not an itemized deduction) and does not help you reach the itemized total.
- So, unless you have significant mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, large medical expenses, or other itemizable items you didn’t list, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction. Standard deduction will be larger than the $2,000 charitable donation alone.

How the home office choice affects itemizing/standard decision
- The home office deduction reduces your self‑employment net income (Schedule C). That lowers your AGI and taxable income, but it does not convert the charitable cash gift into an above-the-line deduction — it still only helps by reducing taxable income overall.
