- Home-office: you can claim a home‑office deduction because you said it’s exclusive and regular. Two methods: simplified ($5/sq ft → $825) or regular (pro rata share of rent and other home expenses → about $6,900). The regular method gives a much larger deduction here but is more complex and can raise other limits/filing requirements (Form 8829).
- Student loan interest: you paid $2,700 but the maximum above‑the‑line deduction is $2,500 (you’d generally be limited to $2,500). At your income level it’s very likely you can take the full $2,500, but that depends on your final MAGI — with the numbers you gave, you’ll probably qualify.
- Charitable gift $2,000: deductible only if you itemize. You have the receipt — that’s good. If you take the standard deduction, you don’t claim it on federal Schedule A.
- Unreimbursed expenses $780: if these are business expenses for your freelance graphic‑design work (not employee unreimbursed expenses), they’re deductible on Schedule C. If they were unreimbursed employee expenses, they generally are not deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension), except very limited categories.

Detailed breakdown and rough calculations
1) Income
- W‑2 income: $79,400
- Self‑employment (graphic design): $11,200
- Interest (Citi savings): $1,780
- E*TRADE: $195
Total gross income ≈ $92,575

2) Home‑office calculation
You said: home office 165 sq ft exclusive; total home 690 sq ft → business-use percent = 165/690 ≈ 23.9%.

Option A — Simplified method
- Deduction = $5 × 165 sq ft = $825.
- Easy to use; no Form 8829.

Option B — Regular method (example using rent only)
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
- Pro rata share = 28,800 × 23.9% ≈ $6,880 (rounded).
- If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs allocated to that percentage, those would add to the deduction.
- To claim this, you normally file Form 8829 (attached to Schedule C).

Note: The regular method usually gives the larger deduction here. However, if your total business deductions (including home office) exceed business income, you may create or increase a business loss which can have additional tax/at‑risk implications. If you want, I can run both scenarios in more detail.

3) Self‑employment tax and related adjustments
- Net business income = $11,200 − (business expenses + home‑office deduction).
- If you use simplified method and also deduct $780 business expenses:
  - Net ≈ 11,200 − 825 − 780 = $9,595.
  - Self‑employment (SE) tax ≈ $1,356 (estimate).
  - You can deduct half of SE tax (~$678) as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- If you use regular method and home‑office ≈ $6,880 plus $780 expenses:
  - Net ≈ 11,200 − 6,880 − 780 = $3,540.
  - SE tax will be much smaller (~$499); half SE tax deductible ≈ $250.

4) Student loan interest
- You reported $2,700 paid. The maximum above‑the‑line deduction for 2024 is $2,500 (on Schedule 1). You can claim up to $2,500 subject to MAGI phaseouts. With your reported income (≈$92.6k gross), you will likely be under the MFJ phaseout threshold and can take the full $2,500 — confirm the interest was actually paid in 2024 and you have Form 1098‑E or payment records.

5) Charitable contribution
- $2,000 cash donation: deductible on Schedule A only if you itemize. Since the standard deduction for MFJ (2024) is $29,200, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction unless you have substantial other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state and local taxes up to limit, large medical expenses, etc.). You mentioned you have the receipt — keep it (required for $250+ cash gifts).

6) Schedule B
- You have interest of $1,780 + $195 = $1,975 total. Because total interest and ordinary dividends exceed $1,500, you should complete Schedule B (Form 1040).

7) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- You may be eligible for the Section 199A QBI 20% deduction on qualified business income from your self‑employment. With the relatively low business income shown, you would likely get up to ~20% of your QBI (after expenses). This reduces taxable income (not AGI) and is subject to limits and calculations. It’s worth including if you have net profit.

8) Estimated tax/withholding
- Because you have self‑employment income, you likely owe self‑employment tax and additional income tax beyond W‑2 withholding. If you expect a tax liability not covered by withholding, you should make estimated quarterly payments (Form 1040‑ES) to avoid underpayment penalties.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — to report deductible part of SE tax and student loan interest
- Schedule B — if interest/dividends > $1,500
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report freelance graphic design income and expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular home‑office method (not used for simplified method)
- Form 1040‑ES — for quarterly estimated tax payments (if needed)
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely from numbers given)
- (If you claim QBI) Form 8995 or 8995‑A (QBI deduction worksheet) — usually Form 8995 for smaller taxpayers

Forms/notes for California state return
- California Form 540 (or 540NR as appropriate) and the CA schedules for income adjustments. California generally follows federal treatment for most business items but has differences — be sure to include your Schedule C profit/loss on the CA return as well and check CA rules for student loan interest and QBI.

Recordkeeping & practical steps
- Keep receipts for the $2,000 charitable donation (written acknowledgement from the charity), documentation for the $2,700 student loan interest (Form 1098‑E or payment records), bank statements for interest, and records for the two freelance clients (invoices, 1099‑NEC if issued).
- For the home office, retain measurements and evidence of exclusive use and the allocation calculations (rent, utilities, insurance, etc.).
- If your clients paid you $600+ each, they may issue 1099‑NEC; income is taxable whether or not you receive a 1099.
- Consider using the simplified home‑office method if you want to keep things simple ($825). Use regular method if you want to maximize the deduction, but be prepared to complete Form 8829 and handle limits.

Recommendation
- Most likely take the standard deduction ($29,200) for 2024 unless you have a mortgage interest or large state/local taxes or other additional deductible items not listed.
- For the home office, if you want the larger immediate deduction and are comfortable with the extra paperwork, use the regular method (Form 8829). If you prefer simplicity and lower audit/complexity risk, use the simplified method ($825).
- Make sure to report freelance income on Schedule C, include business expenses ($780), compute SE tax (Schedule SE), claim half SE tax as an adjustment, claim student loan interest up to $2,500 (Schedule 1), and complete Schedule B for interest reporting.
- Consider paying estimated taxes for 2025 if 2024 withholding didn’t cover your total tax.

Would you like:
- a) an itemized estimate with numbers filled in for both the simplified and regular home‑office methods (I can show AGI, taxable income, and approximate federal income tax and SE tax for each scenario), or
- b) I can draft a checklist of documents/receipts to collect before preparing or sending to your CPA?

Tell me which and I’ll run the detailed numbers or make the checklist.

