Summary — likely outcome and recommendations
- For federal filing (married filing jointly), with the items you listed you will very likely be better off taking the standard deduction rather than itemizing.
- Your graphic design income is self‑employment income and must be reported on Schedule C (Form 1040). That income is subject to income tax and self‑employment (SE) tax (Schedule SE).
- You can claim a home‑office deduction for the exclusive 165 sq ft space used for your graphic design business. Because you pay substantial rent, the actual (business‑use percentage of rent) method will almost certainly give you a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (Schedule 1) up to $2,500 (not $2,700). Eligibility phases out based on MAGI and filing status — you’ll need to check the MAGI limits for 2024 to confirm full eligibility.
- Charitable cash donations ($2,000) are deductible only if you itemize. With the standard deduction for married filing jointly likely larger than $2,000 + any other itemizable items you listed, you’ll probably not itemize.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (post‑TCJA) are not deductible on your federal return unless you are in a special category (e.g., certain educators, armed forces reservists, etc.). If those $780 are business expenses for your self‑employment work, they are deductible on Schedule C.

Numbers and comparisons (approximate)
- Home business portion = 165 / 690 = 0.23913 (about 23.91%).
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
  - Actual method home‑office share of rent ≈ $28,800 × 23.913% ≈ $6,888.
  - Simplified method = $5 × 165 sq ft = $825.
  - Because $6,888 >> $825, the actual method gives a much larger deduction here.
- Effect on self‑employment taxable income (example scenarios, approximate):
  - If you treat $780 as business expenses too and use the actual rent share: net self‑employment income ≈ $11,200 − $6,888 − $780 = $3,532.
  - If you use the simplified method and include $780 business expenses: net ≈ $11,200 − $825 − $780 = $9,595.
  - Self‑employment tax (approx 15.3% on ~92.35% of net) is much lower under the actual method. So the actual method not only lowers income tax but also SE tax.
- Student loan interest: you listed $2,700 paid. The federal deduction is capped at $2,500 — you can deduct up to $2,500 on Schedule 1 (subject to the MAGI phaseout for MFJ).

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — individual income tax return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — to report additional income and adjustments (student loan interest deduction, deductible part of SE tax).
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — profit or loss from business (report your $11,200 and business expenses including home‑office deduction if you use the simplified method; if you use actual method you still report business income/expenses on Schedule C and separately calculate home‑office share on Form 8829).
- Schedule SE (Form 1040) — self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — expenses for business use of your home (only if you use the actual method — required to compute the actual home‑office deduction).
- Schedule B — if your total interest and ordinary dividends are over $1,500 (your interest of $1,780 + $195 = $1,975 > $1,500) you will need Schedule B.
- Form 8995 or Form 8995‑A — Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income from your Schedule C; whether you get the full deduction depends on taxable income and other factors).
- If you itemize (unlikely here) you would use Schedule A — but based on the amounts you gave, standard deduction will probably be larger.
